WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.327
The method starts from a precise definition of
down-scaling: ideal downsampling,

00:00:04.407 --> 00:00:08.733
which first applies an ideal low-pass filter that
zeros out all frequencies above the new Nyquist limit,

00:00:08.813 --> 00:00:13.140
then subsamples.

00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:17.547
From this the authors derive their central condition,

00:00:17.627 --> 00:00:21.953
Claim 1: a deep net is truly scale-equivariant if
and only if every output frequency term

00:00:22.033 --> 00:00:26.360
depends only on input frequency terms that are
equal or lower.

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:30.767
They then redesign each network component to
satisfy this rule.

00:00:30.847 --> 00:00:35.173
A spatially-local Fourier layer constrains the
kernel so it learns local features while staying equivariant.

00:00:35.253 --> 00:00:39.580
A scale-equivariant non-linearity applies ReLU
frequency-band by frequency-band.

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:43.987
A Fourier pooling layer preserves the same
dependency structure.

00:00:44.067 --> 00:00:48.393
On top of the equivariant feature extractor,

00:00:48.473 --> 00:00:52.800
they add a classifier that predicts once per scale,

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:57.207
sharing a single MLP across scales through Fourier
padding,

00:00:57.287 --> 00:01:01.613
and train it with a hinge consistency loss that
penalizes the model whenever a higher-resolution

00:01:01.693 --> 00:01:06.020
image is predicted worse than its lower-resolution
version.
